\chapter{The list library}
The \api{list} package supports construction and higher-order processing.
\api{Range(start, end)} creates ascending integral values with an inclusive
start and exclusive end. Bounds must be exact integers within the binary64
safe-integer range, and the result is limited to one million elements.

\begin{mullcode}
val L <- G:LoadLib("list") or G:DieWith("$E?");
val values <- L:Range(1,6);
val squares <- L:Map(values, fn(x){ return x*x; });
val even <- L:Filter(squares, fn(x){ return x % 2 == 0; });
val sum <- L:Fold(even, 0, fn(a,x){ return a+x; });
G:Assert(squares == [1,4,9,16,25]);
G:Assert(even == [4,16]);
G:Assert(sum == 20);
G:Assert(L:Push([1],2) == [1,2]);
G:Assert(L:Set([1,2],1,9) == [1,9]);
\end{mullcode}

\api{Map(list, function)} calls the function once per element and collects
its results in order. \api{Filter(list, predicate)} retains elements whose
predicate result is true under MuLL's truth rules. \api{Fold(list, initial,
function)} calls the function with the accumulator followed by the current
element, returning the final accumulator. Empty folds return the initial value.

Callbacks can be script functions or native callables. Their effects occur
in list order, and an error stops the operation immediately. Script callbacks
consume the current interpreter step and call budgets.

\api{Push} appends one value to a copy. \api{Set} replaces one element in a
copy and checks the same index rules as core indexing. These are persistent
updates, not mutable container methods.
